task: optics-zero-tax
agent: opus-optics
started: 2026-09-10
branch: feature/optics-zero-tax
worktree: ../okay-wt-zerotax
spec: specs/optics.md (optics-fuse), the operator's question

why: the operator asked why an optic cannot simply be expanded at
  compile time into the code a person would write. It CAN, and Fuse
  already does — for a lens whose halves are written out. The one
  shape it cannot read is `Lens[S](_.f)`, which is the idiomatic way
  to build a lens in this library, so in practice the fusion is off
  for most code that would want it.

what: the reason was stated as "a macro cannot see through another
  macro's captured call". True, and beside the point: `Focus.impl` is
  an ordinary compile-time function over trees, so Fuse does not need
  the compiler to expand it — Fuse can CALL it. Add that case to
  `plan`, drop the S <: Product bound from `impl` so no cast is
  needed, and measure.

predict: a selector-built lens fuses to the same bytes as a
  hand-written copy (24 B/op, ~1.5 ns), where today it falls back to
  the live optic (48 B/op, 3.1 ns). If it does not, the refutation is
  recorded and the comment in Fuse.scala stays as it is.
